#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078142 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078527 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cc2167e-d318-54d5-8b67-47afbc65f530" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Rule Given by a Mapping Diagram Is a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "6d57991d-4cdb-546b-80c5-4df14f1b385d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1096" :question/problem "Is the following rule a function?\n\n![](images/91/91cddcd85f3b6a9efd0fc898ef2004e9e8310593554142de8c94bc4fd1a0ab40.png)" :question/worked-solution "A rule is a function if and only if *every* input is mapped to *precisely one* output.\n\nIn particular, a rule is *not* a function in the following cases:\n\n- An input value is not assigned to an output value. For example, the following situation is *not allowed* for functions (an input value with no arrow from it):\n\n![](images/bb/bb53865d210d631d78e5ce48aa96ecff99da8995936a085c789c3471ffb9e6a0.png)\n\n \n\n- An input value is assigned to more than one output value. For example, the following situation is *not allowed* for functions (an input value with more than one arrow from it):\n\n![](images/18/18831dc73e561faa69748fed111eb7e1024d35849fb6624594ae08d8c35301ee.png)\n\n \nIn this particular example, the rule *is* a function because *each* input value is assigned *precisely one* output value."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078381 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078528 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b869d240-38f8-52c8-bb87-110d7ac616b8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Rule Given by a Table Is a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "279a2ac1-2577-5ec3-8eb4-8496650c6b54" :question/math-academy-id "e-9905" :question/problem "Which missing $xy$-pair would make the following rule a function?\n\n| **$x$** | $-3$ | $-1$ | ? | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $4$ | ? | $0$ | $2$ | $1$ | $7$ |\n\nI. $(1,2)$\n\nII. $(7,3)$\n\nIII. $(-1,5)$" :question/worked-solution "A rule is a function if and only if every input is mapped to *precisely one* output.\n\n- If we substitute the $xy$-pair $(1,2)$ into our table, we get a function.\n\n| **$x$** | $-3$ | $-1$ | $\\color{blue}1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $4$ | $2$ | $0$ | $2$ | $1$ | $7$ |\n\nNotice that all values in the first row are *distinct* (i.e., different). Every input value is assigned to *precisely one* output value.\n\n- None of the other $xy$-pairs gives rules that are functions. So, for example, substituting the $xy$-pair $(-1,5)$ gives a rule that is *not* a function. \n\n| **$x$** | $-3$ | $\\color{red}-1$ | $\\color{red}-1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $4$ | $5$ | $0$ | $2$ | $1$ | $7$ |\n\nNotice that the first row contains the value $\\color{red}x=-1$ twice. The input value $-1$ is assigned to *more than one* output value ($4$ and $5$)."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492044 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078527 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cc2167e-d318-54d5-8b67-47afbc65f530" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Rule Given by a Mapping Diagram Is a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d8f78f3-9170-5ee2-a54a-196cfab0a582" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Yes, since each output is related to at least one input" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491514} {:answer/id #uuid "3bd68649-b47b-539f-afa8-1d38f4403893" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Not enough information" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491570} {:answer/id #uuid "46641fd3-a924-5689-8b29-6b47d95ad36d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Yes, since each input is assigned to exactly one output" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491587} {:answer/id #uuid "731cb4bc-bbc2-5e95-b79a-9d9f37730208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No, since both sets have different numbers of elements" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491659} {:answer/id #uuid "afd3f74c-2c2e-5a58-b40f-52c2a8f52363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No, since there is at least one output with more than one input" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46641fd3-a924-5689-8b29-6b47d95ad36d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Yes, since each input is assigned to exactly one output" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491587} :answer-field/id #uuid "aea26438-c3d8-515d-8034-8cb5d6f50bde" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a93ce581-ceca-53ce-935d-30328bce90e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-130470" :question/problem "![](images/26/26d5274aedc2f04b31a7c122dd3c23257834936e510f187b018f2394c8b6abd9.png)\n\nIs the above rule a function?" :question/worked-solution "A rule is a function if and only if *every* input is mapped to *precisely one* output.\n\nIn particular, a rule is *not* a function in the following cases:\n\n- An input value is not assigned to an output value. For example, the following situation is *not allowed* for functions (an input value with no arrow from it):\n\n![](images/bb/bb53865d210d631d78e5ce48aa96ecff99da8995936a085c789c3471ffb9e6a0.png)\n\n \n\n- An input value is assigned to more than one output value. For example, the following situation is *not allowed* for functions (an input value with more than one arrow from it):\n\n![](images/18/18831dc73e561faa69748fed111eb7e1024d35849fb6624594ae08d8c35301ee.png)\n\n \n\nIn this particular example, the rule *is* a function because *each* input value is assigned *precisely one* output value."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078528 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b869d240-38f8-52c8-bb87-110d7ac616b8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Rule Given by a Table Is a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dbaa9a3-74ca-52a1-b952-f3cf1d7c97b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491480} {:answer/id #uuid "1fc725ed-3feb-5bd6-a8bc-fb71caadc806" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491520} {:answer/id #uuid "35ecb1c6-c69c-563b-8ca7-792e160663a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-8,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491562} {:answer/id #uuid "9ffbc4a0-d6d9-505f-9699-aeab2f70a91b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491737} {:answer/id #uuid "e9e28e8b-f4cd-5741-a59a-cfa8f69168a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491855}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fc725ed-3feb-5bd6-a8bc-fb71caadc806" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491520} :answer-field/id #uuid "27674089-a4c2-5e56-bbd1-5e9f749f8c16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491898}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4de85998-baf3-5654-8a93-fb6fc65cf489" :question/math-academy-id "q-31695" :question/problem "Which missing $xy$-pair would make the following rule a function?\n\n| **$x$** | $-8$ | $-5$ | $-2$ | ? | $3$ | $6$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $10$ | $7$ | $-1$ | ? | $6$ | $8$ | $15$ |" :question/worked-solution "A rule is a function if and only if every input is mapped to *precisely one* output.\n\n- If we substitute the $xy$-pair $(1,2)$ into our table, we get a function.\n\n| **$x$** | $-8$ | $-5$ | $-2$ | $1$ | $3$ | $6$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $10$ | $7$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $6$ | $8$ | $15$ |\n\nNotice that all values in the first row are *distinct* (i.e., different). Every input value is assigned to *precisely one* output value.\n\n- None of the other $xy$-pairs gives rules that are functions. So, for example, substituting the $xy$-pair $(-2,12)$ gives a rule that is *not* a function. \n\n| **$x$** | $-8$ | $-5$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $3$ | $6$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $10$ | $7$ | $-1$ | $12$ | $6$ | $8$ | $15$ |\n\nNotice that the first row contains the value $x=-2$ twice. The input value $-2$ is assigned to *more than one* output value ($-1$ and $12$)."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492031 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078527 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cc2167e-d318-54d5-8b67-47afbc65f530" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Rule Given by a Mapping Diagram Is a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "045c6200-a555-54f7-bf4a-80441d1ccdad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Yes, since each output is related to at least one input" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491458} {:answer/id #uuid "200d6aab-2013-5bd4-9138-56d7d25f5431" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Not enough information" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491522} {:answer/id #uuid "5895e7b6-6a93-519a-b910-a01d6f8be974" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No, since both sets have different numbers of elements" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491620} {:answer/id #uuid "6c24f5fd-0d38-5e73-aeda-5294b9b679ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No, since there is at least one output with more than one input" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491648} {:answer/id #uuid "f1d29d2f-e6ed-5d7e-a30e-0d159afac303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Yes, since each input is assigned to exactly one output" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491867}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1d29d2f-e6ed-5d7e-a30e-0d159afac303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Yes, since each input is assigned to exactly one output" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491867} :answer-field/id #uuid "498fb75a-0671-58f0-8b7a-c51a0893cf04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "85ad9b13-1cf5-585c-ad88-3c1ab1d51635" :question/math-academy-id "q-31702" :question/problem "![](images/d3/d396fe1ef272d88412cb870fc78975517f32dbb3738f4913e34bdf677daee546.png)\n\nIs the above rule a function?" :question/worked-solution "A rule is a function if and only if *every* input is mapped to *precisely one* output.\n\nIn particular, a rule is *not* a function in the following cases:\n\n- An input value is not assigned to an output value. For example, the following situation is *not allowed* for functions (an input value with no arrow from it):\n\n![](images/bb/bb53865d210d631d78e5ce48aa96ecff99da8995936a085c789c3471ffb9e6a0.png)\n\n \n\n- An input value is assigned to more than one output value. For example, the following situation is *not allowed* for functions (an input value with more than one arrow from it):\n\n![](images/18/18831dc73e561faa69748fed111eb7e1024d35849fb6624594ae08d8c35301ee.png)\n\n \n\nIn this particular example, the rule *is* a function because *each* input value is assigned *precisely one* output value."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078528 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b869d240-38f8-52c8-bb87-110d7ac616b8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Rule Given by a Table Is a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d18e8bbd-0f71-5800-add5-92b4ebc5cf11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076047} {:answer/id #uuid "d33382cf-a277-5ba0-a66f-5f94ff3071d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076048} {:answer/id #uuid "2c290509-977d-5bec-97eb-7a76d83405c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076049} {:answer/id #uuid "2d3aacdf-7192-5507-ac00-772fcb5d061d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076050} {:answer/id #uuid "48447a27-666a-58f3-ba33-701af8ebc7cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076051}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48447a27-666a-58f3-ba33-701af8ebc7cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076051} :answer-field/id #uuid "4afc5af1-157b-5384-b7d7-f5bca76286d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b22f15dc-cbde-5d31-8d71-51f8fb6a253c" :question/math-academy-id "q-37248" :question/problem "Consider a rule between $x$ and $y$ given by the table below.\n\n| **$x$** | $12$ | $9$ | $7$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $15$ | $12$ | $9$ | $6$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The rule is a function\n\nII. The rule is **not** a function\n\nIII. The rule maps the input $x=15$ to the output $y=12$" :question/worked-solution "A rule is a function if and only if every input is mapped to precisely one output.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false, and statement II is true. \n\n| **$x$** | $12$ | $9$ | $7$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $15$ | $12$ | $9$ | $6$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nNotice that the first row contains the value $x=9$ twice. The input value $x=9$ is assigned to *more than one* output value ($12$ and $0$).\n\n- Statement III is false. The input $x=15$ is not mapped to anything.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}]}
